KCRA 3 is committed to bringing Sacramento area viewers the daily news they need, want, and expect. Students working with KCRA 3 Journalists will learn from experienced, and creative professionals. KCRA 3 Newsroom employees are happy to help hard working, determined, and energetic interns, but we will not do the work for you. You��d spend a lot of money if you had to hire a producer, camera operator, audio operator, director, technical director, editor, and teleprompter operator to produce your resume tape. You can get one done, for free, if you work hard and make it happen.
Student Expectations
Each student is expected to set their own goals for their internship. Students first need to determine if television news is in their future. Students will see this is a hard working lifestyle, not just a job, or career. Once you decide you want to make a living in telling great stories, you need to focus on getting a job. News Directors, and hiring managers around the country expect to see a resume tape of some kind. While most people no longer send "tapes", you should get your work on a DVD, or uploaded to your own website. That��s where working with us is critical. There are several departments within KCRA 3 Television. A majority of internship can be found in the newsroom. There are several positions students are welcome to shadow.
Download the Application here.
Intern Positions
*The Assignment Desk: It is the heart of the newsroom. All stories pass through the assignment desk at some point before getting on TV. Assignment editors monitor everything from police scanners to Twitter, to the tiny town newspapers across Northern California. Assignment editor are constantly digging, confirming, and updating stories. They often assign reporters to their stories, set up interviews, get updates, field viewer phone calls, story pitches, satellite offers. They work with our NBC, CNN, and Hearst TV partners to share stories and resources. They are master at multitasking, and are still friendly to the life-long viewer who calls for a personal forecast.
*Live Field Crews: Live crews typically consist of a photographer and reporter. On occasion, you have the luxury of an engineer (truck operator) to run your live shot. Reporters and photographers work as a team. They are assigned the story, and get moving. They shoot all video, ask tough questions. They often are interviewing people at the worst moment in their life, and need to be understanding and fair. Live crews are always ready for breaking news, and ready to upgrade their story. They are prepared to cover the house fire, political scandal, and rain all in the same day. They edit their elements, feed it back to the station, and get on TV to tell compelling stories, in an active, creative live shot. The do it in less than two minutes. Interns wanting to focus on photography can expect to learn image composition, framing, sequencing, proper lighting techniques and how to record clean audio.
*Producers/Writers: If you have a controlling personality, producing might be for you. Producers "own" the newscast. Producers decide the lead story, and how it will be told. How much time does the reporter get? What��s the best sound bite? How do you make everyone care? What sidebars compliment the lead story? Will graphics help? Do I move the anchors to a different location? What��s the weather like today? Producers line up the newscast, write all day, and are ready to throw it all for breaking news. The control room is a very exciting, and unpredictable place during a live newscast. Very rarely does a show air entirely as it was produced.
*Editing: Editors are the last stop before we put video on TV. They work with CNN, NBC, and Hearst to gather and edit content for the daily newscasts. They take in feeds from live crews in the field, and get video pushed to the rundown. They record live pictures from around the world, and get the video turned for KCRA 3 viewers almost instantly. KCRA Video Editing interns will learn all aspects of non-linear editing, and some tape-to-tape editing.
*Control Room Production: The control room is made up of at least 4 people, a producer, director, technical director and audio operator. The Director calls the shots, literally. S/he preps the shows, decides which cameras to use and when, routes live pictures on TV. The TD is the button pusher. S/he makes the cool affects you see on TV. They follow the director��s commands. Both positions listen to what is being said on air, and often ditch the plan when there is breaking news.
*Creative Services/Promotions: This department creates and sells our image to the public. They decide which events KCRA 3 will sponsor, and which community service events we will take part in. All graphics are managed by the creative services department. Anything with a KCRA 3 logo on it has been approved by this department. They often help write, and edit ��Opens�� for Specials.
*Digital Media: There are no deadlines when it comes to kcra.com. Everything is Right Now. We do not wait for the next newscast. Web interns should expect to write copy, and help with Friday night High School Playbook Coverage. Interns also are needed for events where they will gather and upload pictures to U-Local.
*Business/Sales Office: Television news is a business. KCRA 3, KQCA My58, and ME TV have a lot of commercials to sell. That��s how we make money. News and sales do not often mix.
